1
00:00:00,110 --> 00:00:05,455
When analysis, source material, and
review decisions live in different places,

2
00:00:05,495 --> 00:00:09,610
teams can lose track of
why a recommendation was made.

3
00:00:09,650 --> 00:00:13,550
The challenge isn't
just generating more information.

4
00:00:13,590 --> 00:00:18,725
It's making that information usable:
what should someone inspect, what supports

5
00:00:18,765 --> 00:00:22,970
the recommendation, and what needs a human decision?

6
00:00:23,010 --> 00:00:28,725
I built Video Insight Bundler to
explore that workflow using video analysis.

7
00:00:28,765 --> 00:00:33,640
My central design choice was to
keep recorded signals, source references, and

8
00:00:33,680 --> 00:00:37,380
human decisions separate, but connected.

9
00:00:37,420 --> 00:00:39,510
Video is the example here;

10
00:00:39,550 --> 00:00:45,205
the broader design problem is connecting
evidence to decisions people can inspect.

11
00:00:45,245 --> 00:00:51,105
This demonstration uses saved
analysis, with original media excluded.

12
00:00:51,145 --> 00:00:56,560
Here, we're reviewing the opening
fifteen seconds of one example video.

13
00:00:56,600 --> 00:01:02,855
The timeline locates the segment, and
its context shows what the analysis recorded.

14
00:01:02,895 --> 00:01:07,285
I'll compare those signals with
the linked recommendation: inspect whether the

15
00:01:07,325 --> 00:01:11,430
opening needs a clearer
transition or a pacing review.

16
00:01:11,470 --> 00:01:15,765
These signals guide inspection, not conclusions.

17
00:01:15,805 --> 00:01:20,235
Before editing, I'd check the original
media and decide whether the opening's

18
00:01:20,275 --> 00:01:25,465
structure is intentional. The workflow lets
me document that question and its

19
00:01:25,505 --> 00:01:31,455
next step. For this example, I
leave the editorial decision open and record

20
00:01:31,495 --> 00:01:36,785
what I checked, the uncertainty,
and what to inspect next.

21
00:01:36,825 --> 00:01:40,930
Now the next reviewer
has an explicit starting point.

22
00:01:40,970 --> 00:01:44,200
This static version requires an external save;

23
00:01:44,240 --> 00:01:50,050
notes don't autosave. I can export
the note and checklist together, keeping the

24
00:01:50,090 --> 00:01:53,100
review record alongside the package.

25
00:01:53,140 --> 00:01:57,975
That makes the handoff inspectable,
even while the editorial decision remains

26
00:01:58,015 --> 00:02:03,790
open. Video is the example,
not the limit of the approach.

27
00:02:03,830 --> 00:02:08,190
A similar workflow could be
designed around research documents, engineering

28
00:02:08,230 --> 00:02:11,720
evidence, or internal review processes.

29
00:02:11,760 --> 00:02:16,980
For a client, I'd start with
the decision, the available inputs, and the

30
00:02:17,020 --> 00:02:21,915
constraints, then determine what to build,
how it should connect to existing

31
00:02:21,955 --> 00:02:26,300
tools, and how we'd evaluate whether it is useful.

32
00:02:26,340 --> 00:02:30,590
If your team has scattered analysis,
or an idea that needs to become

33
